<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: #ffffff; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Dance!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> One Step In, Two Steps Out!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: concept: Use gestures and drawings to link daily routines to classroom activities that are performed.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use gestures and drawings to express movement actions (e.g., jumping up and down). Can clarify or ask for repetition when unsure about movement instructions (e.g., 'Repeat, please.'). Can act out scenes from picture books or songs involving dance movements (e.g., making a dance movement seen in the video of a song).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Movement Simon Says" game. The teacher says simple commands like "Simon says: 'jump up and down!'" or "Simon says: 'take one step forward!'" Students perform the actions. The teacher includes vocabulary like 'step', 'jump', 'move', 'spin', 'turn', 'fast', 'slow'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of daily routines (e.g., "eating breakfast," "sleeping," "brushing teeth"). Then, the teacher models how to link these to classroom movements using gestures and simple phrases. For instance, showing "sleeping" flashcard and then slowly *standing up* while saying "slowly stand up". Showing "running" flashcard and then *hopping* while saying "hop hop hop". The teacher emphasizes gestures as a way to explain or show actions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we use our hands to show 'jump'?" (Yes, teacher jumps with hands).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can we draw a 'step'?" (Yes, teacher draws a foot on the board).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short animated video or picture sequence (e.g., 'Do the Hokey Pokey' or a simple dance tutorial for kids) showing clear, sequential movement commands. The focus is on visual input and simple audio commands. The material should highlight "First, next, then" and numbers one to ten with movement verbs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Acting out dance commands: Follow simple movement instructions in dance, like 'step forward' and 'clap'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays a short segment of the video/picture sequence. Students point to the corresponding movement (e.g., "Where is 'two steps'?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher performs a movement from the video (e.g., 'spin around'). Students identify and say the action (e.g., "Spin!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "Is it 'one step' or 'two steps'?" after a visual prompt, students respond with the correct number and movement.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using sequential commands with numbers (e.g., 'First, take one step to the side.' 'Next, take two steps to the side.'), simple directive phrases (e.g., 'Move to the front.' 'Step to the side.'), and positional commands with prepositions (e.g., 'Stand next to the chair.' 'Jump over the line.').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Teacher-led Mediation:</b> The teacher calls out a command (e.g., "First, take three steps forward!"), performing the action. Students mediate by repeating the command chorally and performing the action with the teacher. The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /t/ in 'two' and /d/ in 'dance'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Drawing and Acting:</b> Students receive simple cards with numbers and verbs (e.g., '2', 'jump'; '1', 'spin'). In pairs, one student shows their card (e.g., '2', 'jump'). The other student draws a simple stick figure jumping two times and then performs "two jumps" for their partner, mediating the instruction through drawing and action. They can use 'J' for 'jump' or 'S' for 'spin' (phonemic awareness).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Command Relay:</b> Students sit in a circle. The teacher starts by giving a command to the first student (e.g., "Juan, move one step to the side."). Juan performs the action, then gestures and says the same command to the next student (e.g., "Maria, move one step to the side."). This continues around the circle. Encourage polite expressions like 'after you' and 'go ahead'.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Fluency focus and communicative task completion)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative group presentation of a "Dance & Count Activity." Students will work in small groups (3-4 children) to create and teach a simple dance sequence. They will use gestures, simple verbal commands, and drawings to mediate their dance steps to their classmates.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Each group will design a short 3-step dance using vocabulary like 'step', 'jump', 'spin', 'turn', 'in', 'out', 'around', and numbers one to three. They will draw their sequence on a large paper (e.g., "First, two steps forward. Next, jump up. Then, spin around.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">During the presentation, one student from the group will show a drawing of the first step and use gestures to explain, "First, take one step forward!" The other group members perform. Then the next student explains the next step. Other classmates are encouraged to ask for repetition if unsure: "Repeat, please."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment and self-reflection)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their "Dance & Count Activity" for the class, mediating the instructions using gestures and drawings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: square; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of movement commands (e.g., /t/ in 'two', /d/ in 'dance').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'step', 'jump', 'spin', numbers 1-3).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of gestures to explain movements.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of drawings to support explanation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to clarify or respond to "Repeat, please." requests.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Group participation and use of polite expressions ('after you', 'go ahead').</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students show a thumbs up if they "Can show friends a dance move with my hands!" and a thumbs down if "It was hard to show my friends." (Focus on gestural mediation).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite dance move to show?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or a little hard to make your friends understand your dance?" (Focus on mediation challenge).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The teacher considers if students effectively used gestures and drawings to mediate movement instructions. Did they ask for and provide clarification successfully? Were the instructions clear enough for Kinder level? What adjustments are needed for future mediation activities (e.g., more structured drawing templates, peer modeling)?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This is the final lesson of the theme. Students have consolidated their understanding of dance movements and numbers through various skills. The next theme will introduce new vocabulary and a new scenario, building on their ability to understand and communicate simple instructions and ideas.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>